 sections:[
 {id:"s1",h:"1. The Church as Mystical Body",html:`<p>\"As the whole Church is termed one mystic body from its likeness to the natural body of a man… so likewise Christ is called the Head of the Church\" (III q.8 a.1), by <strong>order</strong> (he is first), <strong>perfection</strong> (he has the fullness of grace) and <strong>power</strong> (he gives grace to the members).</p>
<p><strong>Members in different degrees.</strong> Christ is Head of all men, but in different ways: of the blessed by glory, of the just by charity, of believers by faith, of those not yet united to him by potency to be united (q.8 a.3).</p>
<div class="co bridge"><div class="co-h">Thomistic bridge</div><p>Pius XII (Mystici Corporis, 1943) and Lumen Gentium 7 develop Thomas's image. The Church is not merely an organisation but Christ's body, animated by the Holy Spirit, who is called the \"soul\" of the Church (CCC 797); Thomas compares him to the heart (q.8 a.1 ad 3).</p></div>`,
  check:Q("Christ is Head of the Church by…",[["order, perfection and power","Correct: q.8 a.1."],["election only","No."],["seniority","No."]],0)},
 {id:"s2",h:"2. Subsists in: the one Church and the many communities",html:`<p>\"This Church constituted and organized in the world as a society, subsists in the Catholic Church, which is governed by the successor of Peter and by the Bishops in communion with him, although many elements of sanctification and of truth are found outside of its visible structure\" (LG 8).</p>
<p><strong>Elements outside.</strong> \"The written word of God; the life of grace; faith, hope and charity, with the other interior gifts of the Holy Spirit, and visible elements too\" can exist outside the visible boundaries; \"all of these, which come from Christ and lead back to Christ, belong by right to the one Church of Christ\" (UR 3).</p>
<div class="co stance"><div class="co-h">[Stance] What \"subsists\" means</div><p>The CDF (Responses, 2007) explained that \"subsists\" means the perduring, historical continuity and permanence of all the elements instituted by Christ in the Catholic Church, in which the Church of Christ is concretely found. It does not mean the Church of Christ is equally present in all communities, nor that it is absent elsewhere.</p></div>`,
  check:Q("According to UR 3, elements of the Church found outside its visible boundaries…",[["belong by right to the one Church of Christ","Correct."],["are worthless","No."],["make all communities equal","No."]],0)},
 {id:"s3",h:"3. No salvation outside the Church: what it means",html:`<p>The ancient axiom <em>extra ecclesiam nulla salus</em> (Cyprian; Lateran IV; Florence) is taught today in LG 14:</p>
<div class="quote">"Whosoever, therefore, knowing that the Catholic Church was made necessary by Christ, would refuse to enter or to remain in it, could not be saved."<span class="src">Lumen Gentium 14</span></div>
<p>Thomas's sacramental version: the reality (res) of the Eucharist is \"the unity of the mystical body, without which there can be no salvation\" (III q.73 a.3); yet one can have that reality by desire before receiving the sacrament. So those who, through no fault of their own, do not know Christ or his Church, but seek God sincerely and try to do his will under grace, \"may achieve eternal salvation\" (LG 16), always through Christ and in relation to the Church.</p>
<div class="co warn"><div class="co-h">[Warn] Two errors</div><ul><li><strong>Rigorism</strong> (Leonard Feeney, excommunicated 1953): all non-Catholics are damned. The Holy Office letter of 1949 rejected this reading.</li><li><strong>Indifferentism</strong>: all religions are equal paths. Rejected by Dominus Iesus (2000).</li></ul></div>`,
  check:Q("Who, according to LG 14, could not be saved?",[["Anyone not visibly Catholic","Too broad."],["Those who know the Church is necessary and refuse to enter or remain","Correct."],["Catechumens","No."]],1)},
 {id:"s4",h:"4. Pope and bishops; infallibility",html:`<p><strong>Collegiality.</strong> The bishops, with and never without the pope, form a college that succeeds the apostles (LG 22). Bishops teach infallibly when, dispersed or gathered in council, in communion with the pope, they agree that a doctrine is to be held definitively (LG 25).</p>
<p><strong>Papal infallibility</strong> (Vatican I, 1870): conditions, paraphrased:</p>
<ol><li>The pope speaks <em>ex cathedra</em>: as shepherd and teacher of all Christians;</li>
<li>by his supreme apostolic authority;</li>
<li>he defines a doctrine of faith or morals;</li>
<li>to be held by the whole Church.</li></ol>
<p>Then he enjoys the infallibility Christ willed for his Church; such definitions are irreformable \"of themselves, and not from the consent of the Church\". Infallibility is not impeccability, nor inspiration, nor a guarantee of every papal statement.</p>
<p><strong>Thomas</strong> anticipates the principle: a new edition of the Creed belongs to the pope's authority, \"to whom the more important and more difficult questions that arise in the Church are referred\" (II-II q.1 a.10).</p>`,
  check:Q("Which is NOT a condition of papal infallibility?",[["Speaking as teacher of all Christians","It is."],["Defining a doctrine of faith or morals","It is."],["The pope's personal holiness","Correct: not a condition."],["To be held by the whole Church","It is."]],2)},
 {id:"s5",h:"5. The four marks",html:`<div class="tablewrap"><table class="t"><thead><tr><th>Mark</th><th>Meaning</th><th>Thomas / sources</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td>One</td><td>one faith, one baptism, one government</td><td>Eph 4:5; II-II q.39 a.1 (schism opposes unity)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Holy</td><td>sanctified by Christ, with means of holiness, despite sinful members</td><td>Eph 5:26–27; CCC 823–829</td></tr>
<tr><td>Catholic</td><td>universal in place, time and fullness of means</td><td>CCC 830–856</td></tr>
<tr><td>Apostolic</td><td>founded on the apostles, keeping their teaching, governed by their successors</td><td>Irenaeus, Against Heresies III.3</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
<div class="co apply"><div class="co-h">[Apply] South African ecumenism</div><p>In South Africa Catholics work with other churches in the South African Council of Churches (SACC); the SACBC is a member. Ecumenism seeks full communion in truth (UR 4), not a lowest common denominator.</p></div>`,
  check:Q("Schism is opposed to which mark?",[["Holiness","No."],["Unity","Correct: II-II q.39 a.1."],["Apostolicity","Indirectly."]],1)}
 ],
